Test Case 2:

For a fixed reflection coefficient magnitude (K=0.5) and varying load impedances (ZL={25 92,50 22,100 22,150 2}), calculate the position of voltage
minima nearest to the load. Validate that the position of Vmin changes with the phase angle of T, which is influenced by the load impedance.

AIM: To calculate the position of voltage minima nearest to the load For a fixed reflection coefficient magnitude (K=0.5) and varying load impedance values.

CODE:
clc;
clear;
close;
Zo = 50;
f = 300*(10^6);
// Wavelength
lo = 3*(10^8)/f;
// Fixed reflection coefficient magnitude
ampK = 0.5;
// Load impedance values (polar form)
ZL_mag = [25 50 100 150];
ZL_ang = [92 22 22 2];
printf("Load Impedance\tSWR\tVoltage Minimum (m)\n");
printf("---------------------------------------------\n");
for i = 1:4
// Convert polar impedance into rectangular form
ZL = ZL_mag(i)(cos(ZL_ang(i)%pi/180) + %isin(ZL_ang(i)%pi/180));
// Reflection coefficient
K = (ZL-Zo)/(ZL+Zo);
// Phase angle
phi = atan(imag(K),real(K));
// Fixed magnitude with calculated phase
T = ampK*(cos(phi)+%i*sin(phi));
// SWR
S = (1+abs(T))/(1-abs(T));
// Position of nearest voltage minimum
ymin = modulo((phi+%pi)lo/(4%pi),lo/2);
printf("%d angle %d\t%f\t%f\n", ZL_mag(i), ZL_ang(i), S, ymin);
end

